= 1H
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Number Theory}

= Solution
{parent=1H}

The <Euler totient function> counts integers $1\leq a\leq n$ with $\gcd(a,n)=1$. The <Möbius function> has $\mu(1)=1$, is zero when a prime square divides $n$, and equals $(-1)^r$ when $n$ is a product of $r$ distinct primes. Consequently
$$
\sum_{d\mid n}\mu(d)=\begin{cases}1&n=1,\\0&n>1,\end{cases}
$$
because for $n>1$ the sum over squarefree divisors is $(1-1)^r$.

The <Möbius inversion formula> is **$g(n)=\sum_{d\mid n}\mu(d)f(n/d)$**. Substituting the divisor sum defining $f$ and collecting the coefficient of $g(e)$ gives
$$
\sum_{d\mid n}\mu(d)f(n/d)=\sum_{e\mid n}g(e)\sum_{d\mid n/e}\mu(d)=g(n).
$$
This proof uses only finite sums, so no convergence hypothesis is needed.

Classifying $1,\ldots,n$ by their greatest common divisor with $n$ gives $\sum_{d\mid n}\varphi(d)=n$: the class with greatest common divisor $n/d$ contains exactly $\varphi(d)$ integers. Applying the <Möbius inversion formula> yields
$$
\boxed{\varphi(n)=\sum_{d\mid n}\mu(d)\frac nd=n\prod_{p\mid n}(1-p^{-1}).}
$$
The <Riemann zeta function> is $\zeta(s)=\sum_{n\geq1}n^{-s}$ for $\operatorname{Re}s>1$. <Absolute convergence> justifies multiplication of <Dirichlet series>. The divisor identity for the <Möbius function> gives $\sum\mu(n)n^{-s}=1/\zeta(s)$ there. For $\operatorname{Re}s>2$ both factors below converge absolutely, and their <Dirichlet convolution> is the <Euler totient function>:
$$
\boxed{\frac{\zeta(s-1)}{\zeta(s)}=\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{\varphi(n)}{n^s}.}
$$

= 2I
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Topics in Analysis}

= i
{parent=2i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

A degree-$n$ <Legendre polynomial> $P_n$ is orthogonal, for integration on $[-1,1]$, to every <polynomial> of degree at most $n-1$, and has $n$ distinct roots in $(-1,1)$. Divide $p$ by $P_n$:
$$
p=P_nq+r,\qquad \deg q\leq n-1,\quad\deg r\leq n-1.
$$
The <orthogonality of Legendre polynomials> gives $\int p=\int r$. At every quadrature node $x_j$, $p(x_j)=r(x_j)$. The assumed exactness for $r$ therefore proves **exactness up to degree $2n-1$**, the characteristic property of <Gaussian quadrature>.

= ii
{parent=2i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Use the <Lagrange interpolation polynomial>
$$
\ell_j(t)=\prod_{i\ne j}\frac{t-x_i}{x_j-x_i},\qquad \ell_j(x_i)=\delta_{ij}.
$$
Its square has degree $2n-2$, so the exactness already proved gives
$$
\boxed{A_j=\int_{-1}^1\ell_j(t)^2\,dt>0.}
$$
The <positive weights of Gaussian quadrature> follow because $\ell_j$ is a nonzero continuous <polynomial>. Thus these <Gaussian quadrature> weights are in fact positive, rather than merely nonnegative.

= iii
{parent=2i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Apply the <Gaussian quadrature> rule to the constant <polynomial> $1$. Exactness gives **$\sum_{j=1}^n A_j=2$**.

= Solution
{parent=2I}

To prove <convergence of positive quadrature on continuous functions>, for a continuous $f$, the <Weierstrass approximation theorem> supplies a <polynomial> $p$ with $\|f-p\|_\infty<\varepsilon$. For every sufficiently large $n$, the <Gaussian quadrature> rule is exact for this fixed <polynomial>. Positivity of its weights and their sum $2$ then give
$$
\left|Q_n(f)-\int_{-1}^1f\right|\leq\sum_jA_j|f(x_j)-p(x_j)|+\int_{-1}^1|f-p|\leq4\varepsilon.
$$
Since $\varepsilon$ is arbitrary, **$Q_n(f)\to\int_{-1}^1f$**. Notice that no differentiability of $f$ is needed; uniform <polynomial> approximation and positivity provide the whole argument.

= 3G
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Coding and Cryptography}

= Solution
{parent=3G}

The <Shannon entropy>, in bits, is
$$
H(A)=-\sum_ap_a\log_2p_a=\log_2 5-\frac15\approx\boxed{2.121928}.
$$
For a prescribed alphabet distribution and code alphabet, an <optimal code> is a <uniquely decodable code> minimizing expected codeword length. <Huffman coding> produces an optimal binary <prefix code>, and its minimum is also the minimum among binary <uniquely decodable codes>.

Two optimal binary <prefix codes>, listing words in the order $a,b,c,d,e$, are
$$
(00,01,10,110,111),\qquad(0,10,110,1110,1111).
$$
Their length vectors are $(2,2,2,3,3)$ and $(1,2,3,4,4)$, and both have **expected length $2.2$ bits**. To verify optimality rather than just equal length, run <Huffman coding>. First combine $d,e$ into a node of weight $0.2$. Among the resulting three weight-$0.2$ nodes, combining $b,c$ and then combining the remaining weight-$0.2$ node with one weight-$0.4$ node gives the first length vector. Instead combine $c$ with the $d,e$ node, then combine that weight-$0.4$ node with $b$, and finally with $a$; this gives the second. Each step combines two smallest current weights, so both constructions are <Huffman coding> trees. Ties therefore allow **different optimal length profiles**.

= 4J
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Statistical Modelling}

= Solution
{parent=4J}

Writing $y_i$ for the observed counts, independence of the <Poisson distributions> gives the <log-likelihood>
$$
\ell(\beta)=\sum_{i=1}^n\left\{y_i\beta x_i-e^{\beta x_i}-\log(y_i!)\right\}.
$$
Its <score function> and negative second derivative are
$$
U(\beta)=\sum_ix_i(y_i-e^{\beta x_i}),\qquad J(\beta)=\sum_ix_i^2e^{\beta x_i}.
$$
Unless all $x_i$ vanish, $J(\beta)>0$, so the <log-likelihood> is strictly concave. Use <Newton method>, starting from a finite $\beta_0$, with
$$
\boxed{\beta_{r+1}=\beta_r+\frac{\sum_ix_i(y_i-e^{\beta_rx_i})}{\sum_ix_i^2e^{\beta_rx_i}}.}
$$
Stop when the <score function> or the change in $\beta$ is small; a reduced step can ensure that the <log-likelihood> increases. Any finite zero of the <score function> is the unique <maximum-likelihood estimator>.

Existence needs a qualification. If the nonzero $x_i$ include both signs, $U$ decreases from $+\infty$ to $-\infty$, so a finite root exists. If all nonzero $x_i$ are positive, a finite root exists exactly when $\sum_ix_iy_i>0$; otherwise the supremum is approached as $\beta\to-\infty$. The corresponding condition for all negative $x_i$ is $\sum_ix_iy_i<0$, with a supremum at $+\infty$ otherwise. When all $x_i=0$, the parameter is unidentifiable. These cases explain when the iterative algorithm actually has a finite estimate to find.

= 5E
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Mathematical Biology}

= Solution
{parent=5E}

At a positive spatially homogeneous <equilibrium point>, $v=u^2$ and $2+u-v=0$. Hence **$(u,v)=(2,4)$**. The reaction <Jacobian matrix> there is
$$
J=\begin{pmatrix}2&-2\\4a&-a\end{pmatrix},\qquad\operatorname{tr}J=2-a,\quad\det J=6a.
$$
The <two-dimensional Routh-Hurwitz stability criterion> gives **strict linear asymptotic stability for $a>2$**, and instability for $0<a<2$.

For a spatial mode with $s=k^2$, the reaction-diffusion <linearization> is $J_s=J-\operatorname{diag}(s,ds)$. When $a>2$, its trace is negative for every $s\geq0$, while
$$
D(s)=\det J_s=ds^2+(a-2d)s+6a.
$$
A growing mode exists precisely when this quadratic is negative at some $s>0$. Its minimum must occur at positive $s$, and must be strictly negative, giving
$$
2d>a,\qquad(2d-a)^2>24ad.
$$
With $z=d/a$, the latter inequality becomes $4z^2-28z+1>0$. Selecting the root compatible with $2d>a$ yields the <Turing instability> threshold
$$
\boxed{d>\left(\frac72+2\sqrt3\right)a.}
$$
The unstable band is
$$
\frac{2d-a-\sqrt{(2d-a)^2-24ad}}{2d}<k^2<\frac{2d-a+\sqrt{(2d-a)^2-24ad}}{2d}.
$$
On a bounded domain a boundary condition must permit a mode in this band. Equality at the threshold gives a neutral mode, not positive growth.

At $a=2$, the homogeneous <linearization> has purely imaginary <eigenvalues>, so it is not strictly linearly asymptotically stable. If “stable” is read as nonlinear stability, the boundary deserves attention. Put $x=u-2$, $y=(v-4-x)/\sqrt3$, and $x=r\cos\theta$, $y=r\sin\theta$. The exact equations give $\dot r=r^2F(\theta)$, $\dot\theta=\omega+rG(\theta)$, where $\omega=2\sqrt3$,
$$
F=-\frac{\cos^2\theta\sin\theta}{\sqrt3}+\cos\theta\sin^2\theta,\qquad G=\frac{2\cos^3\theta}{\sqrt3}+\cos^2\theta\sin\theta+\sqrt3\cos\theta\sin^2\theta.
$$
Expanding $dr/d\theta$ through cubic order, $\int_0^{2\pi}F=0$ cancels the quadratic displacement. The correction from the quadratic change in $r$ also integrates to zero, because it is proportional to $(\int F)^2$. Since $\int_0^{2\pi}FG=\pi/(2\sqrt3)$, the <Poincaré return map> is
$$
r\longmapsto r-\frac{\pi}{24\sqrt3}r^3+O(r^4).
$$
This <cubic return-map stability of a weak focus> shows that $a=2$ is a weak attracting focus. The strict linear range used in the <Turing instability> calculation is $a>2$; the positive homogeneous equilibrium is also nonlinearly asymptotically stable at the boundary $a=2$. For that boundary, $\operatorname{tr}J_s=-(1+d)s<0$ for every nonzero spatial mode. The same strict <Turing instability> threshold and unstable band therefore also apply there, with the homogeneous mode understood through nonlinear stability.

= 6B
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Further Complex Methods}

= Solution
{parent=6B}

<Analytic continuation> extends a <holomorphic function> by holomorphic elements agreeing on overlaps; along a path, a chain of overlapping neighbourhoods transports the initial element. The <identity theorem> makes compatible continuation unique on connected overlaps, although different paths can lead to different branches.

Choose the principal <complex logarithm> and the slit domain $D=\mathbb C\setminus[1,\infty)$. On $D$, the function
$$
h(z)=-\frac{\operatorname{Log}(1-z)}z
$$
is holomorphic, with the removable value $h(0)=1$. The domain $D$ is simply connected, so $h$ has a primitive and its integral from $0$ to $z$ is independent of the path in $D$. For $|z|<1$, the uniformly convergent <power series> on compact subdiscs gives
$$
f(z)=\int_0^zh(u)\,du=\int_0^z\sum_{n\geq1}\frac{u^{n-1}}n\,du=\sum_{n\geq1}\frac{z^n}{n^2}.
$$
Therefore **$f$ is a single-valued <analytic continuation> of the <dilogarithm> to $\mathbb C\setminus[1,\infty)$**. The apparent problem at $0$ is removable; $1$ is a genuine branch point, even though the function has a finite one-sided limit there, because its derivative has a logarithmic singularity. Other cuts from $1$ to infinity give corresponding branches.

= 7D
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Classical Dynamics}

= a
{parent=7d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The two cyclic <Euler angles> give conserved <canonical momenta>
$$
L_3=p_\psi=I_3(\dot\psi+\dot\phi\cos\theta),\qquad L_z=p_\phi=I_1\dot\phi\sin^2\theta+L_3\cos\theta.
$$
Here $L_3$ is the body-axis <angular momentum> and $L_z$ the vertical <angular momentum>. Thus **$\omega_3=L_3/I_3$ is constant**. Since the <Lagrangian> has no explicit time dependence, the <energy> is also conserved:
$$
E=\frac{I_1}2\dot\theta^2+\frac{I_1}2\dot\phi^2\sin^2\theta+\frac{L_3^2}{2I_3}+Mgl\cos\theta.
$$
These are the three independent conserved quantities furnished by the continuous symmetries of the <heavy symmetric top>.

= b
{parent=7d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Eliminate the cyclic velocities using $\dot\phi=(L_z-L_3\cos\theta)/(I_1\sin^2\theta)$. The conserved <energy> becomes
$$
E-\frac{L_3^2}{2I_3}=\frac{I_1}2\dot\theta^2+V_{\rm eff}(\theta),\qquad\boxed{V_{\rm eff}=\frac{(L_z-L_3\cos\theta)^2}{2I_1\sin^2\theta}+Mgl\cos\theta.}
$$
Differentiating the <effective potential> gives
$$
-\frac{dV_{\rm eff}}{d\theta}=I_1\dot\phi^2\sin\theta\cos\theta-L_3\dot\phi\sin\theta+Mgl\sin\theta,
$$
which is also the $\theta$ <Euler-Lagrange equation>. Hence **$I_1\ddot\theta=-V_{\rm eff}'(\theta)$**. Deriving it directly from the <Euler-Lagrange equation> ensures it also holds at turning points, where division by $\dot\theta$ in an <energy> argument would be invalid.

= c
{parent=7d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For nonvertical <uniform precession of a heavy symmetric top>, write $\dot\phi=\Omega$ and fix $\theta$. The $\theta$ <Euler-Lagrange equation> reduces to
$$
\boxed{I_1\cos\theta\,\Omega^2-I_3\omega_3\Omega+Mgl=0.}
$$
For $\cos\theta\ne0$ its solutions are
$$
\Omega=\frac{I_3\omega_3\pm\sqrt{I_3^2\omega_3^2-4I_1Mgl\cos\theta}}{2I_1\cos\theta}.
$$
For $0<\theta<\pi/2$, uniform <precession> therefore requires **$|\omega_3|\geq2\sqrt{I_1Mgl\cos\theta}/I_3$**. For $\pi/2<\theta<\pi$, the discriminant is positive for every <spin>, including zero. At $\theta=\pi/2$, the condition is $\Omega=Mgl/(I_3\omega_3)$, requiring nonzero <spin>. Once this condition holds, choose $L_z=I_1\Omega\sin^2\theta+L_3\cos\theta$ and $\dot\psi=\omega_3-\Omega\cos\theta$; both cyclic equations are then satisfied. The exactly vertical configurations are also solutions for arbitrary <spin>, but the azimuthal member of the <Euler angles> is degenerate there, so their existence is separate from this nonvertical discriminant condition.

= 8C
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Cosmology}

= Solution
{parent=8C}

The flat matter-dominated <Friedmann equation> gives $\rho=1/(6\pi Gt^2)$ when $a=t^{2/3}$. With $P=C\rho^{4/3}$,
$$
c_s^2=\frac{dP}{d\rho}=\frac{4C}3\rho^{1/3}=\bar c_s^2t^{-2/3},\qquad\bar c_s^2=\frac{4C}3(6\pi G)^{-1/3}.
$$
For <linear density modes with polytropic pressure>, substitution gives the <Euler-Cauchy equation>
$$
\ddot\delta+\frac4{3t}\dot\delta+\frac{\bar c_s^2k^2-2/3}{t^2}\delta=0.
$$
A <power law> has exponent satisfying $n(n-1)+4n/3+\bar c_s^2k^2-2/3=0$. Thus, for distinct roots,
$$
\boxed{\delta=A_kt^{n_+}+B_kt^{n_-},\qquad n_\pm=-\frac16\pm\sqrt{\frac{25}{36}-\bar c_s^2k^2}.}
$$
For $k\ll k_J$, the two modes approach $t^{2/3}$ and $t^{-1}$: gravity produces a growing <linear cosmological density perturbation> and a decaying mode. For $k\gg k_J$, put $\nu=\sqrt{\bar c_s^2k^2-25/36}$; the real solution is
$$
\delta=t^{-1/6}\{C_k\cos(\nu\log t)+D_k\sin(\nu\log t)\}.
$$
These are pressure-supported oscillations with a decaying envelope. For the <repeated-root cosmological density mode> $k=k_J$, the complete solution is **$t^{-1/6}(A_k+B_k\log t)$**. The specified $k_J=5/(6\bar c_s)$ separates real exponents from logarithmic oscillations; the growing exponent actually crosses zero at $k=\sqrt{2/3}/\bar c_s$, a slightly smaller value.

= 9I
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Topics in Analysis}

= Solution
{parent=9I}

<Sperner's lemma> says that a triangulated triangle whose vertices are labelled $1,2,3$, with each outer vertex having its own label and each boundary edge using only its endpoint labels, contains a small triangle with all three labels. In fact the number of such triangles is odd. Count incidences of small triangles with edges labelled $1,2$. Interior edges contribute twice. On the outer $1,2$ edge, the number of changes between these labels is odd; the other two outer edges contribute zero. A small triangle contributes one incidence if it has all three labels, two if it uses both $1,2$ but not $3$, and zero otherwise. The total parity proves the assertion.

The resulting <no-retraction theorem> is: **there is no continuous <retraction> of a closed disc onto its boundary**. Work first with a closed triangle $P$. If $r:P\to\partial P$ fixes the boundary, colour each vertex of increasingly fine triangulations by an index of a largest barycentric coordinate of $r(v)$. The largest coordinate is at least $1/3$; on the boundary the colouring satisfies <Sperner's lemma>. Choose a tricoloured small triangle in each triangulation. By <compactness> and shrinking mesh, a subsequence of their three vertices converges to one point $x$. Continuity forces all three barycentric coordinates of $r(x)$ to be at least $1/3$, so $r(x)$ is the triangle's centre, contradicting $r(x)\in\partial P$. A <homeomorphism> of a disc with $P$ proves the disc assertion.

<Brouwer fixed-point theorem> states that every continuous self-map $F$ of a closed disc has a fixed point. If it had none, the ray from $F(x)$ through $x$ would meet the boundary beyond $x$, continuously in $x$. That intersection defines a <retraction>, since a boundary point is already the outgoing intersection. This contradicts the <no-retraction theorem>.

Finally fix $y\in\mathbb R^2$ and define $F_y(x)=y+x-g(x)$. The displacement bound implies $\|F_y(x)-y\|\leq K$, so $F_y$ maps the closed radius-$K$ disc centred at $y$ into itself. <Brouwer fixed-point theorem> gives $F_y(x)=x$, which is exactly $g(x)=y$. Since $y$ was arbitrary, **$g$ is surjective**.

= 10G
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Coding and Cryptography}

= Solution
{parent=10G}

In <RSA>, choose $N=pq$ with distinct large primes, an exponent $e$ coprime to $\varphi(N)$, and $d$ satisfying $ed\equiv1\pmod{\varphi(N)}$. Publish $(N,e)$ and keep $d$ secret. Encrypt a <residue> $m$ as $c=m^e\bmod N$ and decrypt by $c^d\bmod N$. <Fermat's little theorem> on each prime and the <Chinese remainder theorem> prove decryption, including messages not coprime to $N$.

A customer knows a nonzero multiple $M=ed-1$ of $\varphi(N)$. Write $M=2^st$ with $t$ odd. Choose a base $a$ at random. A nontrivial $\gcd(a,N)$ already factors $N$. Otherwise compute $a^t,a^{2t},\ldots,a^{2^st}$ modulo $N$. The last entry is $1$. If the first occurrence of $1$ is preceded by $z\not\equiv\pm1\pmod N$, then
$$
\boxed{\gcd(z-1,N)\text{ is a nontrivial factor of }N.}
$$
Indeed, the two prime components of this square root of $1$ must have opposite signs. This procedure succeeds for some bases: by the <Chinese remainder theorem>, choose a base whose component modulo $p$ has order $2$ and whose component modulo $q$ has order $1$. Its odd $t$-th power is already a nontrivial square root of $1$. Trying bases therefore gives an explicit factoring algorithm, and repeated random choices give the usual efficient attack. More precisely, if the two components' orders have different powers of $2$, the procedure succeeds. For a uniform element of a cyclic group with $2$-part $2^r$, those order valuations have probabilities $2^{-r}$ for $0$ and $2^{j-1-r}$ for $1\leq j\leq r$. Independence of the two prime components shows that equal valuations have probability at most $1/2$. Thus a random unit succeeds with probability at least $1/2$. Once $N$ is factored, every customer's private exponent is computable. The exceptional $ed=1$ is an identity cipher and is already insecure.

For the outsider's <RSA common-modulus attack>, find integers $a,b$ with $ae_1+be_2=1$ by <Bézout's identity>. For a unit message,
$$
\boxed{m\equiv c_1^a c_2^b\pmod N,}
$$
using modular inverses for negative powers. If a ciphertext is a nonzero nonunit, its greatest common divisor with $N$ factors $N$ instead; zero ciphertext corresponds to the zero message for a squarefree modulus. **The common message is recoverable without a private key.** For a generic unit message this alone does not supply the factorization or all private keys, so it does not demonstrate a complete break for unrelated messages. The nonunit case does give that stronger break.

= 11B
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Further Complex Methods}

= Solution
{parent=11B}

For $\operatorname{Re}s>1$, expand $(e^t-1)^{-1}=\sum_{n\geq1}e^{-nt}$. <Absolute convergence> of the integrated series follows from the same expression with $\operatorname{Re}s$ in place of $s$. Integrating termwise and substituting $u=nt$ gives
$$
\int_0^\infty\frac{t^{s-1}}{e^t-1}\,dt=\sum_{n\geq1}n^{-s}\Gamma(s)=\Gamma(s)\zeta(s).
$$
This proves the <Mellin transform> representation of the <Riemann zeta function>.

Take the <Hankel contour> along the lower side of the negative axis towards zero, around zero counterclockwise, and back along the upper side. For $\operatorname{Re}s>1$ the small-circle contribution tends to zero. On the lower and upper rays, respectively, $\arg t=-\pi,+\pi$, so the two integrals combine to
$$
\int_H\frac{t^{s-1}}{e^{-t}-1}\,dt=\left(e^{-i\pi(s-1)}-e^{i\pi(s-1)}\right)\int_0^\infty\frac{x^{s-1}}{e^x-1}\,dx=2i\sin(\pi s)\Gamma(s)\zeta(s).
$$
The <gamma reflection formula> now proves agreement of the two representations. At an integer $s=m\geq2$, the contour integral has a zero while $\Gamma(1-s)$ has a pole: **their product is interpreted by its finite limit, equal to $\zeta(m)$**, not by multiplying literal zero and infinity.

For nonpositive integers the integrand is single-valued, and the contour closes around zero. Since
$$
\frac1{e^{-t}-1}=-\frac1t-\frac12+O(t),
$$
the <residue> of $t^{-1}/(e^{-t}-1)$ is $-1/2$, giving **$\zeta(0)=-1/2$**. Also
$$
\frac1{e^{-t}-1}+\frac12=-\frac12\coth(t/2)
$$
is odd. Its Laurent series therefore has only odd powers. Multiplication by $t^{-2n-1}$ cannot produce a $t^{-1}$ term, and the subtracted constant cannot do so when $n\geq1$. Hence the contour integral vanishes and **$\zeta(-2n)=0$ for every positive integer $n$**.

= 12C
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Classical Dynamics}

= a
{parent=12c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <canonical momentum> is $p=L_{\dot q}$, and a regular <Legendre transform in mechanics> defines $H(q,p,t)=p\dot q-L(q,\dot q,t)$ after solving for $\dot q$. Its differential is
$$
dH=\dot q\,dp-L_q\,dq-L_t\,dt,
$$
because the $d\dot q$ terms cancel. With the <Euler-Lagrange equation> $\dot p=L_q$, this gives <Hamilton's equations>
$$
\boxed{\dot q=H_p,\qquad\dot p=-H_q.}
$$
In particular $H_q=-L_q$ at corresponding velocities, so a <cyclic coordinate> for the <Lagrangian> is also cyclic for the <Hamiltonian>.

= b
{parent=12c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <canonical momenta> are $p_x=m\dot x$, $p_y=m\dot y+qBx$, $p_z=m\dot z$. The <Legendre transform in mechanics> gives
$$
\boxed{H=\frac{p_x^2+(p_y-qBx)^2+p_z^2}{2m}+qEy.}
$$
<Hamilton's equations> are
$$
\dot x=p_x/m,\quad\dot y=(p_y-qBx)/m,\quad\dot z=p_z/m,\qquad\dot p_x=qB\dot y,\quad\dot p_y=-qE,\quad\dot p_z=0.
$$
Thus $\ddot x=\Omega\dot y$, $\ddot y=-\Omega\dot x-qE/m$, and $\ddot z=0$, where $\Omega=qB/m$. The signs also follow from $\mathbf E=-E\mathbf e_y$ and $\mathbf B=B\mathbf e_z$.

For $Bq\ne0$, put $v_d=-E/B$, $a=v_{x0}-v_d$, $b=v_{y0}$. The motion is
$$
\boxed{\begin{aligned}x(t)&=x_0+v_dt+\frac{a\sin\Omega t+b(1-\cos\Omega t)}\Omega,\\y(t)&=y_0+\frac{a(\cos\Omega t-1)+b\sin\Omega t}\Omega,\\z(t)&=z_0+v_{z0}t.\end{aligned}}
$$
Differentiation verifies both equations and all initial conditions. The centre of the circular motion has the <electric drift> <velocity> $-E\mathbf e_x/B$. For $B=0$, take $x=x_0+v_{x0}t$, $y=y_0+v_{y0}t-qEt^2/(2m)$ and the same $z$. For $q=0$, the particle moves freely.

= 13I
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Logic and Set Theory}

= a
{parent=13i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The recursive definition of <ordinal addition> is $\alpha+0=\alpha$, $\alpha+(\beta+1)=(\alpha+\beta)+1$, and $\alpha+\lambda=\sup_{\beta<\lambda}(\alpha+\beta)$ for a nonzero <limit ordinal> $\lambda$. The synthetic definition is the order type of the ordered disjoint union of a copy of $\alpha$ followed by a copy of $\beta$.

For the synthetic operation, adding the empty order does nothing, adding a successor appends one final point, and concatenation with a limit order is the increasing union of the initial concatenations with all its proper initial segments. Their order types have supremum $\sup_{\beta<\lambda}(\alpha+\beta)$. Thus the synthetic operation satisfies the recursive equations. <Transfinite induction> gives uniqueness of the recursively defined operation, proving equivalence.

The recursive definitions of <ordinal multiplication> and <ordinal exponentiation> are
$$
\alpha\cdot0=0,\quad\alpha(\beta+1)=\alpha\beta+\alpha,\quad\alpha\lambda=\sup_{\beta<\lambda}\alpha\beta;
$$
$$
\alpha^0=1,\quad\alpha^{\beta+1}=\alpha^\beta\alpha,\quad\alpha^\lambda=\sup_{\beta<\lambda}\alpha^\beta.
$$
The last equation in each line is for nonzero limit $\lambda$. In particular the convention $0^0=1$ belongs to this recursive definition.

= b
{parent=13i}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The first assertion is **false**: $1<2$, but $1+\omega=2+\omega=\omega$. The second is **true**. In the concatenation definition of <ordinal addition>, the copy of $\alpha$ is a proper initial segment of the copy of $\beta$ when $\alpha<\beta$, so $\gamma+\alpha$ is a proper initial segment of $\gamma+\beta$.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

**No.** The definition of <ordinal exponentiation> gives $2^\omega=\sup_n2^n=\omega=\sup_n3^n=3^\omega$, although $2<3$.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

**No such <ordinal> exists.** For $\alpha>1$, <ordinal multiplication> gives $\alpha^2=\alpha\alpha\geq\alpha\cdot2=\alpha+\alpha>\alpha$. Since <ordinal exponentiation> is nondecreasing in the exponent for base $\alpha>1$, $\alpha^\omega\geq\alpha^2>\alpha$.

= iv
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

For every countable <ordinal> $\beta$, $\omega^\beta$ is countable: successor stages use a countable product, and countable limit stages a countable union. Hence $\omega^{\omega_1}=\sup_{\beta<\omega_1}\omega^\beta\leq\omega_1$. Conversely $\omega^\beta\geq\beta$ by <transfinite induction>, so these values are cofinal in $\omega_1$. Therefore **$\omega^{\omega_1}=\omega_1$**.

It is not the least fixed point. Set $a_0=1$, $a_{n+1}=\omega^{a_n}$ and define <epsilon zero> as $\varepsilon_0=\sup_na_n$. This is countable, and continuity of <ordinal exponentiation> at limits gives $\omega^{\varepsilon_0}=\sup_n\omega^{a_n}=\varepsilon_0$. Any fixed point of $\alpha\mapsto\omega^\alpha$ dominates every $a_n$ by induction, hence dominates $\varepsilon_0$. Thus **the least fixed point is $\varepsilon_0<\omega_1$**.

= 14I
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Graph Theory}

= a
{parent=14i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <graph Ramsey number> $R(s,t)$ is the least $N$ for which every <graph> on $N$ vertices has a <clique> of size $s$ or an <independent set> of size $t$; $R(s)=R(s,s)$ is the <diagonal Ramsey number>. The base cases are $R(2,t)=t$ and $R(s,2)=s$.

Assume the smaller parameters already have finite <graph Ramsey numbers>, and take $N=R(s-1,t)+R(s,t-1)$. For any vertex, either its neighbourhood has at least $R(s-1,t)$ vertices, or its nonneighbourhood has at least $R(s,t-1)$ vertices. In the first case it contains an independent $t$-set, or an $(s-1)$-clique which extends with the chosen vertex. In the second it contains an $s$-clique, or a $(t-1)$-independent set which extends with that vertex. Induction proves existence and
$$
\boxed{R(s,t)\leq R(s-1,t)+R(s,t-1).}
$$

= b
{parent=14i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Induction with Pascal's identity gives the <binomial upper bound for a Ramsey number> $R(s,t)\leq\binom{s+t-2}{s-1}$. Thus $R(s)\leq\binom{2s-2}{s-1}\leq4^{s-1}$, proving **$R(s)=O(4^s)$**.

For the lower bound, take a <binomial random graph> $G(n,1/2)$. The expected total number of $s$-cliques and independent $s$-sets is
$$
2\binom ns2^{-\binom s2}\leq2\frac{n^s}{s!}2^{-s(s-1)/2}.
$$
For $n=\lfloor2^{s/2}/2\rfloor$, this is at most $2^{1-s/2}/s!<1$ for all sufficiently large $s$. Some <graph> therefore has neither forbidden set, so $R(s)>n$. This proves **$R(s)=\Omega(2^{s/2})$** by the <probabilistic method>.

= c
{parent=14i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The <binomial upper bound for a Ramsey number> gives $R(3,t)\leq\binom{t+1}{2}=O(t^2)$. For the <triangle-free Ramsey lower bound by alteration>, a <binomial random graph> $G(n,p)$ has expected triangle count $\binom n3p^3$ and expected independent $t$-set count $\binom nt(1-p)^{\binom t2}$. Choose a realization whose sum of these counts is at most its expectation. Delete one vertex from each surviving forbidden set, iterating through the originally listed sets. The remaining <graph> has neither a triangle nor an independent $t$-set. This <random alteration method> proves
$$
R(3,t)>n-\binom n3p^3-\binom nt(1-p)^{\binom t2}.
$$
For large $t$, set $n=\lfloor(t/(4\log t))^{3/2}\rfloor$ and $p=n^{-2/3}$. The triangle term is at most $n/6$. Using $\binom nt\leq(en/t)^t$ and $1-p\leq e^{-p}$, the logarithm of the independent-set term divided by $t$ is at most
$$
\log(en/t)-\frac{p(t-1)}2\leq\frac12\log t-\frac32\log\log t+O(1)-2\log t+o(1),
$$
which tends to $-\infty$. That term is eventually below $1$. Thus $R(3,t)>5n/6-1$ and
$$
\boxed{R(3,t)=\Omega\!\left((t/\log t)^{3/2}\right).}
$$

= 15F
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Representation Theory}

= i
{parent=15f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <symmetric power> $S^nV$ is the quotient of $V^{\otimes n}$ identifying tensors which differ by permutation of the factors. The <exterior power> $\Lambda^nV$ is the alternating quotient, with a tensor having repeated equal factors set to zero. Both quotients inherit the given group action. For a <basis> $e_1,\ldots,e_d$, bases consist respectively of monomials $e_1^{a_1}\cdots e_d^{a_d}$ with $\sum a_i=n$, and wedge products $e_{i_1}\wedge\cdots\wedge e_{i_n}$ with $i_1<\cdots<i_n$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\dim S^nV=\binom{d+n-1}{n},\qquad\dim\Lambda^nV=\binom dn,}
$$
with the second dimension zero for $n>d$.

A finite-order group element is diagonalizable over $\mathbb C$, because its minimal <polynomial> divides $X^m-1$, which has no repeated roots. Choose an <eigenbasis> with <eigenvalues> $\lambda_1,\ldots,\lambda_d$. The induced <eigenvalues>, including multiplicities, are
$$
\boxed{\lambda_1^{a_1}\cdots\lambda_d^{a_d}\ (a_i\geq0,\ \sum a_i=n)\text{ on }S^nV;\quad\lambda_{i_1}\cdots\lambda_{i_n}\ (i_1<\cdots<i_n)\text{ on }\Lambda^nV.}
$$
The zeroth powers are the one-dimensional trivial representation, with the empty product equal to $1$.

= ii
{parent=15f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <character of a representation> is its trace. The <exterior power> character is the elementary symmetric <polynomial> of degree $n$ in the <eigenvalues>, so the specified sign convention $Q(g,X)=\det(g-XI)$ gives
$$
\boxed{Q(g,X)=\sum_{n=0}^d(-1)^{d-n}\chi_{\Lambda^nV}(g)X^{d-n}.}
$$
To obtain the <character generating series of symmetric powers>, sum the monomial <eigenvalues> over all degrees. As an identity of <formal power series>,
$$
\boxed{\sum_{n\geq0}\chi_{S^nV}(g)t^n=\prod_{j=1}^d(1-\lambda_jt)^{-1}=\frac1{(-t)^dQ(g,t^{-1})}.}
$$
The apparent negative powers cancel in the denominator, which has constant term $1$. Equivalently, this series is the reciprocal of $\sum_{j=0}^d(-1)^j\chi_{\Lambda^jV}(g)t^j$. Comparing coefficients gives $\sum_{j=0}^{\min(d,n)}(-1)^j\chi_{\Lambda^jV}(g)\chi_{S^{n-j}V}(g)=0$ for $n>0$, an explicit recurrence computing the symmetric-power characters from $Q$.

= 16H
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Number Fields}

= i
{parent=16h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Use the customary squarefree convention for the quadratic radicand $d$. For $d\equiv2,3\pmod4$, the <ring of integers of a number field> is $\mathbb Z[\sqrt d]$, so
$$
\mathcal O_{\mathbb Q(\sqrt d)}/(p)\cong\mathbb F_p[X]/(X^2-d).
$$
The ideal $(p)$ is prime exactly when this quotient is an <integral domain>. A <polynomial> quotient of a field is an <integral domain> exactly when the defining <polynomial> is irreducible. This proves the assertion, including the possibility of ramified primes where the reduction has a repeated root.

Squarefreeness is necessary for the printed assertion as written: $d=18\equiv2\pmod4$ defines the same field as $d=2$. The prime $3$ is inert in $\mathbb Q(\sqrt2)$ because $X^2-2$ is irreducible over $\mathbb F_3$, although $X^2-18=X^2$ is reducible. Thus **the result requires a squarefree quadratic radicand**.

= ii
{parent=16h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Put $s=\sqrt{-14}$. Then $\mathcal O_K=\mathbb Z[s]$ and the <field discriminant> is $-56$. Reduction of $X^2+14$ modulo $2$ and $3$ gives
$$
\boxed{(2)=\mathfrak p_2^2,\ \mathfrak p_2=(2,s);\qquad(3)=\mathfrak p_3\overline{\mathfrak p}_3,\ \mathfrak p_3=(3,s-1),\ \overline{\mathfrak p}_3=(3,s+1).}
$$
Their <ideal norms> are $2,3,3$, respectively. The <Minkowski bound for ideal classes> is $(2/\pi)\sqrt{56}<5$, so every ideal class has an integral representative of <norm> at most $4$. The only possible representatives are the unit ideal, $\mathfrak p_2$, $\mathfrak p_3$, $\overline{\mathfrak p}_3$, and the norm-$4$ ideal $\mathfrak p_2^2=(2)$. Thus there are at most four classes.

The class of $\mathfrak p_2$ is nontrivial, since a <principal ideal> of <norm> $2$ would require $x^2+14y^2=2$. Also $\mathfrak p_3^2$ is not principal: <norm> $9$ forces $x+ys=\pm3$, whose ideal is $\mathfrak p_3\overline{\mathfrak p}_3$, not $\mathfrak p_3^2$.

Now $N(2+s)=18$. It is divisible by $\mathfrak p_2$ and $\mathfrak p_3$, but not by $\overline{\mathfrak p}_3$, as substitution of $s=-1$ modulo $3$ gives $1$. Unique ideal factorization and <norms> therefore give $(2+s)=\mathfrak p_2\mathfrak p_3^2$. Consequently $[\mathfrak p_3]^2=[\mathfrak p_2]^{-1}=[\mathfrak p_2]$, a nontrivial class of order $2$. The class of $\mathfrak p_3$ has order $4$, and the four-class bound is attained:
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Cl}(\mathbb Q(\sqrt{-14}))\cong C_4,\text{ generated by }[(3,\sqrt{-14}-1)].}
$$

= 17F
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Galois Theory}

= i
{parent=17f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <fundamental theorem of Galois theory> gives an inclusion-reversing bijection between <subgroups> $H\leq\operatorname{Gal}(L/K)$ and intermediate fields $M$, by $H\mapsto L^H$ and $M\mapsto\operatorname{Gal}(L/M)$, for a finite <Galois extension>. One has $[L:L^H]=|H|$ and $[L^H:K]=[\operatorname{Gal}(L/K):H]$. The field $L^H$ is Galois over $K$ exactly when $H$ is normal; then its Galois group is the quotient by $H$.

Let $\alpha=\sqrt[3]2$ and $\zeta=e^{2\pi i/3}$. The <splitting field> is $L=\mathbb Q(\alpha,\zeta)$. A <splitting field> in <characteristic zero> is normal and separable, hence Galois. <Eisenstein criterion> at $2$ gives $[\mathbb Q(\alpha):\mathbb Q]=3$. This field is real, whereas $\zeta$ is not, and $\zeta$ satisfies a quadratic <polynomial>. Thus $[L:\mathbb Q]=6$. The faithful permutation action on the three roots identifies the Galois group with $S_3$. The <subgroup> fixing $\alpha$ is generated by the transposition interchanging the other two roots. Its conjugates are different transposition <subgroups>, so **it is not normal**.

= ii
{parent=17f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <cyclotomic polynomial> is $\Phi_8(t)=t^4+1$. Over $\mathbb F_7$,
$$
\boxed{\Phi_8(t)=(t^2+3t+1)(t^2-3t+1),}
$$
since the middle coefficient in the product is $2-9=-7=0$. Both factors have positive degree, proving reducibility. In fact their discriminant $5$ is not a square modulo $7$, so these are the two irreducible quadratic factors.

= iii
{parent=17f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Write $K=\mathbb Q(\mu_m)$, $L=\mathbb Q(\mu_n)$. For coprime $m,n$, <Bézout's identity> and powers of a primitive $mn$-th root give $KL=\mathbb Q(\mu_{mn})$. The degrees of these <cyclotomic fields> are $\varphi(m)$, $\varphi(n)$ and $\varphi(mn)=\varphi(m)\varphi(n)$. The natural restriction map
$$
\operatorname{Gal}(KL/\mathbb Q)\longrightarrow\operatorname{Gal}(K/\mathbb Q)\times\operatorname{Gal}(L/\mathbb Q)
$$
is injective and, by these orders, bijective. Under this product description the <subgroups> fixing $K$ and $L$ are the two complementary factors. Together they generate the whole group, so their common fixed field is $\mathbb Q$. By the <fundamental theorem of Galois theory>, **$K\cap L=\mathbb Q$**. This also includes $m=1$ or $n=1$.

= 18H
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Algebraic Topology}

= Solution
{parent=18H}

A <covering map> is a continuous surjection $p:\widetilde X\to X$ for which each point has an open neighbourhood $U$ with $p^{-1}(U)$ a disjoint union of open sets, each mapped homeomorphically onto $U$. A connected simply connected <covering space> is a <universal cover>.

There is a necessary connectedness qualification in the printed conclusion. Let $A_0$ be the path component of $a_0$ in $A$. Then $p(\widetilde A)=A_0$: one inclusion follows by projecting paths, and the other by lifting a path in $A_0$ from $a_0$. Thus the asserted surjection onto all of $A$ requires $A$ to be path connected. Without it, take $p$ to be the identity of $\mathbb R$ and $A$ to be two disjoint intervals; a selected component cannot cover both.

To prove the assertion about a <component of a pulled-back universal cover>, choose an evenly covered neighbourhood $U$ in $X$. Local path connectedness of $A$ supplies a path-connected relatively open neighbourhood $V\subseteq U\cap A_0$. Each inverse sheet over $V$ is path connected, so it lies wholly in $\widetilde A$ or is disjoint from it. These sheets evenly cover $V$ under $p|_{\widetilde A}$. Therefore **$p|_{\widetilde A}:\widetilde A\to A_0$ is a <covering map>**.

Under the <classification of connected covering spaces>, its <subgroup> consists of based loops in $A_0$ whose lifts close at $\widetilde a_0$. A loop viewed in $X$ closes in the <universal cover> exactly when its class in $\pi_1(X,a_0)$ is trivial. Hence the <subgroup> is
$$
\boxed{p_*\pi_1(\widetilde A,\widetilde a_0)=\ker\{\pi_1(A,a_0)\xrightarrow{i_*}\pi_1(X,a_0)\}.}
$$
Here $\pi_1(A,a_0)=\pi_1(A_0,a_0)$, so the <subgroup> formula itself needs no change. Injectivity of the covering's induced fundamental-group map is the usual homotopy-lifting property.

= 19G
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Linear Analysis}

= a
{parent=19g}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

A <linear map> $T:X\to Y$ between normed spaces is a <bounded linear operator> if $\|Tx\|\leq C\|x\|$ for one finite $C$ and every $x$. This implies $\|Tx-Ty\|\leq C\|x-y\|$, hence continuity. Conversely, continuity at zero supplies $\delta>0$ such that $\|x\|<\delta$ implies $\|Tx\|<1$. Apply this to $\delta x/(2\|x\|)$ to obtain $\|Tx\|\leq2\|x\|/\delta$ for nonzero $x$. Thus **boundedness and continuity are equivalent for <linear maps>**.

The <operator norm> is $\|T\|=\sup_{\|x\|\leq1}\|Tx\|$, equivalently $\sup_{x\ne0}\|Tx\|/\|x\|$. The bounded operators form a <vector space>, since $\|(S+T)x\|\leq(\|S\|+\|T\|)\|x\|$ and scalar multiples remain bounded. Taking suprema proves the <triangle inequality> and absolute homogeneity for the <operator norm>; $\|T\|=0$ forces $Tx=0$ for every $x$. Thus $B(X,Y)$ is a normed space, whether or not $X,Y$ are complete.

= b
{parent=19g}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Take $f_n(t)=\sin(2\pi nt)$. Its domain <norm> is $1$, whereas its image <norm> is $1+2\pi n$. Hence **$T$ is unbounded**, so it has no finite <operator norm> and is not a <compact operator>.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For the inclusion, $\|Tf\|_\infty\leq\|f\|_\infty+\|f'\|_\infty$, with equality for a nonzero constant. Thus **$\|T\|=1$**. The domain unit ball is uniformly bounded and has $\|f'\|_\infty\leq1$, so it is <equicontinuous>. The <Arzela-Ascoli theorem> makes its image relatively compact in $C[0,1]$. Therefore **$T$ is compact**.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Differentiation satisfies $\|Tf\|_\infty\leq\|f\|$, so its <operator norm> is at most $1$. With $f_n(t)=\sin(2\pi nt)/(2\pi n)$, the ratio $\|Tf_n\|_\infty/\|f_n\|$ tends to $1$. Hence **$\|T\|=1$**.

Normalize $g_n=f_n/(1+(2\pi n)^{-1})$, so $\|g_n\|=1$. Their derivatives are $\cos(2\pi nt)/(1+(2\pi n)^{-1})$. Distinct cosine functions are orthogonal in $L^2(0,1)$, and these normalizing factors stay bounded away from zero. Consequently the derivative images have pairwise $L^2$ distances bounded away from zero. Since <uniform convergence> would imply $L^2$ convergence, no subsequence converges uniformly. Thus **$T$ is not compact**.

= iv
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

The integration functional satisfies $|Tf|\leq\|f\|_\infty\int_0^1|h|$. If $h$ never vanishes, its sign is constant and $f=\operatorname{sgn}h$ attains equality. In general use the <continuous functions> $f_\varepsilon=h/\sqrt{h^2+\varepsilon}$, of supremum <norm> at most $1$. Then
$$
Tf_\varepsilon=\int_0^1\frac{h^2}{\sqrt{h^2+\varepsilon}}\,dt\longrightarrow\int_0^1|h(t)|\,dt
$$
by the <dominated convergence theorem>. Therefore **$\|T\|=\int_0^1|h|$**. Every bounded subset of its finite-dimensional target $\mathbb R$ is relatively compact, so **$T$ is compact**, including when $h=0$.

= 20F
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Riemann Surfaces}

= Solution
{parent=20F}

Two <holomorphic function> elements represent the same <germ of a holomorphic function> at $z$ if they agree on some neighbourhood of $z$. For a holomorphic $f$ on an open $D\subseteq G$, set $U(f,D)=\{[f]_z:z\in D\}$. These sets form a <basis of a topology> on the <space of germs of holomorphic functions>: every germ lies in one, and if a germ lies in two, equality of the representatives on a smaller neighbourhood supplies a <basis of a topology> set in their intersection. Declaring arbitrary unions of <basis of a topology> sets open therefore gives a topology.

Different base points are separated by disjoint small discs in $G$. If two different germs have the same base point, choose representatives on one connected small disc. Their sheets are disjoint: any common germ elsewhere on that disc would force equality throughout the disc by the <identity theorem>. This proves the Hausdorff property.

The projection $\pi([f]_z)=z$ maps each sheet $U(f,D)$ homeomorphically onto $D$. Use these maps as complex charts. On overlaps their transition functions are the identity, hence holomorphic; thus each connected component is a <Riemann surface>, and $\pi$ is a local <biholomorphism>, an <analytic germ projection>. For completeness, second countability of a component follows by following continuation chains through a countable <basis of a topology> of rational discs: starting at one germ, finite chains of such discs give only countably many continuation sheets, by uniqueness on overlaps, and they cover every germ in that component.

A <complete analytic function> consists of all germs reachable from one initial germ by <analytic continuation>. The supplied path-lifting equivalence identifies these with its path component. Since the germ space is locally path connected, connected components and path components agree. Thus its continuation surface is exactly one component $\mathcal G_{\mathcal F}$.

A local <biholomorphism> need not evenly cover the whole base; the germ projection is an analytic covering in this local sense. The examples below distinguish a genuine <regular covering map> from a surjective nonregular projection, with the meaning of regular stated explicitly.

= i
{parent=20f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Take $G=\mathbb C^*$ and the <complete analytic function> generated by a logarithm germ. Its continuation surface identifies with $\mathbb C$ via $w\mapsto[\log]_z$ with $z=e^w$ and logarithm value $w$. Thus $\pi(w)=e^w$. It is a <universal cover>; the deck transformations $w\mapsto w+2\pi in$ act transitively on each fibre, so it is also a <regular covering map> in the deck-transitive sense. **It is not bijective**, since every nonzero $z$ has infinitely many logarithm values. It also meets the alternative convention in which “regular” merely requires an evenly covered neighbourhood at each base point.

= ii
{parent=20f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

A <surjective inverse-polynomial germ projection without even covering> comes from the inverse of $P(w)=w^3-3w$ over $G=\mathbb C$. Consider $R=\mathbb C\setminus\{-1,1\}$, with $\pi(w)=P(w)$. Since $P'(w)=3(w^2-1)\ne0$ on $R$, each $w$ defines a holomorphic inverse germ of $P$ at $P(w)$. This identifies $R$ with a component of the <space of germs of holomorphic functions>: inverse germs continue along paths in $R$, and the holomorphic identity $P(f(z))=z$ persists under continuation. A germ with value $\pm1$ cannot exist, because differentiating that identity would give $P'(\pm1)f'(z)=1$. Since $R$ is connected, all its inverse germs belong to one <complete analytic function>.

The projection is surjective. A generic value has three distinct regular preimages; the critical values $2,-2$ each retain a simple regular preimage, respectively $2,-2$, even after their double critical preimages $-1,1$ are removed. Thus fibre size drops from $3$ to $1$ at these values, making an evenly covered neighbourhood impossible. This is **surjective but not a regular topological covering**.

If “regular” is specifically used for deck transitivity among genuine coverings, there is also a genuine nonnormal example. Remove the critical values from the base and all their preimages from the source:
$$
G=\mathbb C\setminus\{-2,2\},\qquad R=\mathbb C\setminus\{-2,-1,1,2\},\qquad\pi(w)=w^3-3w.
$$
This is a connected three-sheeted analytic covering. A small loop about each critical value swaps the two roots meeting there. The two swaps are distinct and generate $S_3$ (connectedness also forces transitive monodromy). The stabilizer of one sheet is an order-$2$ <subgroup>, which is not normal in $S_3$. The <classification of connected covering spaces> therefore shows **this covering is not regular in the deck-transitive sense**. Either convention has a fully explicit example.

= 21F
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Algebraic Geometry}

= i
{parent=21f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <radical of an ideal> $I$ in a commutative ring $A$ is $\sqrt I=\{a\in A:a^m\in I\text{ for some }m\geq1\}$. It is an ideal: if $a^m,b^n\in I$, every term of $(a+b)^{m+n}$ has either at least $m$ factors $a$ or at least $n$ factors $b$, so the sum belongs to $I$. Also $(ca)^m\in I$ for every $c\in A$, and additive inverses preserve the defining condition.

= ii
{parent=21f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

If $f\in\sqrt I$, a power $f^m$ vanishes at every point of $Z(I)$, so $f$ itself vanishes there. This gives $\sqrt I\subseteq I(Z(I))$.

For the reverse inclusion, take $f$ vanishing on $Z(I)$ and use the <Rabinowitsch trick>. In $k[x_1,\ldots,x_n,t]$ form $J=(I,1-tf)$. A common zero would have $x\in Z(I)$, whence $f(x)=0$ and $1-tf(x)=1$, a contradiction. The assumed <weak Hilbert Nullstellensatz> gives $J=(1)$. Thus there is a finite expression
$$
1=\sum_ja_j(x,t)i_j(x)+b(x,t)(1-tf(x)),\qquad i_j\in I.
$$
If $f=0$ the conclusion is immediate. Otherwise substitute $t=f^{-1}$ in the localized <polynomial> ring. The last term vanishes, and clearing a sufficiently high power of $f$ gives $f^N\in I$. Hence $f\in\sqrt I$, proving the <strong Hilbert Nullstellensatz>:
$$
\boxed{I(Z(I))=\sqrt I.}
$$

= iii
{parent=21f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

If a power of $a$ lies in $I\cap J$, then $a\in\sqrt I\cap\sqrt J$. Conversely, if $a^m\in I$ and $a^n\in J$, the power $a^{\max(m,n)}$ lies in both ideals. Therefore **$\sqrt{I\cap J}=\sqrt I\cap\sqrt J$** for any commutative ring.

= iv
{parent=21f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

**The equality is false.** In $A=k[x,y]$, take $I=(y)$ and $J=(y-x^2)$. Both are <prime ideals>, since their quotient rings are isomorphic to $k[x]$, so both are radical. But
$$
\sqrt I+\sqrt J=(y,x^2),\qquad\sqrt{I+J}=(x,y).
$$
The element $x$ belongs to the latter ideal and not the former. This also shows why taking the <radical of an ideal> can enlarge a sum of already radical ideals.

= 22G
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Differential Geometry}

= Solution
{parent=22G}

An <immersion> $\phi:U\to\mathbb R^3$ has differential of rank $2$, equivalently <linearly independent> $\phi_u,\phi_v$. It has <isothermal coordinates> when its <first fundamental form> has $E=G>0$, $F=0$. A <minimal surface> has zero <mean curvature>.

For an isothermal <immersion>, differentiation of $E=G$ and $F=0$ gives
$$
(\phi_{uu}+\phi_{vv})\cdot\phi_u=\tfrac12E_u+F_v-\tfrac12G_u=0,
$$
and similarly its scalar product with $\phi_v$ is zero. Its normal component is $e+g$. The given <mean curvature> formula reduces to $H=(e+g)/(2E)$, so
$$
\boxed{\phi_{uu}+\phi_{vv}=2EH\mathbf n.}
$$
Thus an isothermal <immersion> is minimal exactly when each coordinate is a <harmonic function>.

An explicit example is the <Enneper surface>, parametrized on $\mathbb R^2$ by
$$
\phi(u,v)=(u-u^3/3+uv^2,\ v-v^3/3+u^2v,\ u^2-v^2).
$$
Every coordinate has zero Laplacian. Direct differentiation gives $\phi_u\cdot\phi_v=0$ and $|\phi_u|^2=|\phi_v|^2=(1+u^2+v^2)^2>0$. Thus it is an isothermal <immersion> and is minimal everywhere.

It is not an open subset of any of the three listed surfaces. The parameter points $(\sqrt3,0)$ and $(-\sqrt3,0)$ both map to $(0,0,3)$, but their tangent planes differ: $\phi_u=(-2,0,\pm2\sqrt3)$ while $\phi_v=(0,4,0)$. A plane, <catenoid>, or <helicoid> is an embedded smooth surface with a unique tangent plane at each point. If our whole image lay in any one of them, both rank-$2$ tangent planes at this point would have to equal its tangent plane, a contradiction.

= 23J
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Probability and Measure}

= a
{parent=23j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For $1\leq p<\infty$, $L^p(E,\mathcal E,\mu)$ consists of <measurable functions> with $\int_E|f|^p\,d\mu<\infty$, identifying functions equal <almost everywhere>. Its <Lp norm> is $\|f\|_p=(\int|f|^p)^{1/p}$. Thus membership is a condition on an almost-everywhere <equivalence class>, not on individual point values.

= b
{parent=23j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

<Hölder's inequality> states that for conjugate exponents $1/p+1/q=1$, $f\in L^p$ and $g\in L^q$ imply $fg\in L^1$ and
$$
\boxed{\int|fg|\,d\mu\leq\|f\|_p\|g\|_q.}
$$
It includes $p=1,q=\infty$ (and the reversed case), with the essential-supremum <norm> for $L^\infty$.

= c
{parent=23j}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

By the <Cauchy-Schwarz inequality>, the integral is bounded by $\|f\|_2\|k_x\|_2$, where $k_x(y)=|x-y|^{-\alpha}$. For $0\leq x\leq1$,
$$
\int_0^1|x-y|^{-2\alpha}\,dy=\frac{x^{1-2\alpha}+(1-x)^{1-2\alpha}}{1-2\alpha}<\infty.
$$
For $x$ outside the interval, the integral is bounded by the value at the nearest endpoint; the endpoint value is finite as well. Since $2\alpha<1$, the kernel is square-integrable for every real $x$, proving **<absolute convergence> for every $x$**.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Subtract the two absolutely convergent integrals and apply the <Cauchy-Schwarz inequality> to $f$ and the difference of the kernels. This gives precisely
$$
\boxed{|g(x+h)-g(x)|\leq\|f\|_2\left(\int_0^1\bigl(|x+h-y|^{-\alpha}-|x-y|^{-\alpha}\bigr)^2\,dy\right)^{1/2}.}
$$

= iii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

For $h\ne0$, enlarge the nonnegative integral $I(h)$ to all of $\mathbb R$, and substitute $y=x+|h|t$. Reflection if necessary gives
$$
I(h)\leq |h|^{1-2\alpha}C_\alpha,\qquad C_\alpha=\int_{\mathbb R}\bigl(|t-1|^{-\alpha}-|t|^{-\alpha}\bigr)^2\,dt.
$$
Near $0$ and $1$, its integrand is bounded by a constant times $1+|t|^{-2\alpha}$ or $1+|t-1|^{-2\alpha}$, which is integrable. For large $|t|$, the <mean value theorem> bounds the difference by a constant times $|t|^{-\alpha-1}$, whose square is integrable. Thus $C_\alpha<\infty$. The preceding estimate yields
$$
\boxed{|g(x+h)-g(x)|\leq\|f\|_2\sqrt{C_\alpha}\,|h|^{1/2-\alpha}.}
$$
In particular **$g$ is continuous on all of $\mathbb R$**; the proof establishes <Hölder continuity of a one-dimensional fractional kernel integral> of exponent $1/2-\alpha$ uniformly in $x$.

= 24K
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Applied Probability}

= i
{parent=24k}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

A rate-$\lambda$ <Poisson process> is a counting process starting at zero, with right-continuous nondecreasing integer-valued paths, independent stationary increments, and $N_t-N_s\sim\operatorname{Pois}(\lambda(t-s))$ for $0\leq s<t$. Its jumps have size one almost surely.

For independent <Poisson processes> $N,M$, increments of $N+M$ on disjoint intervals are independent, because all the corresponding increments of the two processes are jointly independent. On an interval of length $t$, multiplying their <probability generating functions> gives
$$
\exp(\lambda t(z-1))\exp(\mu t(z-1))=\exp((\lambda+\mu)t(z-1)).
$$
Thus the increment is Poisson with mean $(\lambda+\mu)t$. The path requirements hold, and independent processes almost surely have no simultaneous jumps. Therefore **$N+M$ is a <Poisson process> of rate $\lambda+\mu$**.

= ii
{parent=24k}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The random clock is understood to be independent of the discrete-time <Markov chain>; without this hypothesis its law need not be determined by $K$ alone. Conditional on $N_t=n$, the transition matrix is $K^n$, so
$$
P_t=e^{-\lambda t}\sum_{n\geq0}\frac{(\lambda t)^n}{n!}K^n=e^{\lambda t(K-I)},\qquad\boxed{Q=\lambda(K-I).}
$$
Hence $q_{xy}=\lambda K_{xy}$ for $x\ne y$ and $q_{xx}=-\lambda(1-K_{xx})$. If $\pi K=\pi$, then $\pi P_t=\pi$ by the series, proving that the <invariant distribution> is unchanged.

Let $\xi_i$ be the independent exponential interarrival times of the <Poisson process>. The absorption time is $T_a=\sum_{i=1}^{\tau_a}\xi_i$. By independence of the chain and clock and <Tonelli theorem>,
$$
\mathbb E_xT_a=\sum_{i\geq1}\mathbb E[\xi_i]\mathbb P_x(\tau_a\geq i)=\boxed{\frac1\lambda\mathbb E_x\tau_a.}
$$
This includes infinite expectations and absorption at time zero. The same argument proves the hinted random-sum identity for an independent nonnegative integer-valued count $M$.

= 25J
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Principles of Statistics}

= Solution
{parent=25J}

For $n\geq1$, the binomial <log-likelihood> is $X\log\theta+(n-X)\log(1-\theta)$ plus a constant. Its maximizer, including endpoints, is **$\widehat\theta_{\rm MLE}=X/n$**. For $0<\theta<1$, the <Fisher information> is
$$
\boxed{I(\theta)=\frac{n}{\theta(1-\theta)}.}
$$
This follows either from the expected negative second derivative or the <variance> of $(X-n\theta)/(\theta(1-\theta))$. At $0,1$ the usual regular score formula does not apply; the interior information diverges as an endpoint is approached.

For a symmetric <beta distribution> prior $\operatorname{Beta}(s,s)$, conjugacy gives $\theta\mid X\sim\operatorname{Beta}(X+s,n-X+s)$, with <posterior mean> $(X+s)/(n+2s)$. All three priors below are instances of this calculation.

= i
{parent=25j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <uniform prior> has $s=1$, so the posterior is $\operatorname{Beta}(X+1,n-X+1)$. Its mean and mode are
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E[\theta\mid X]=\frac{X+1}{n+2},\qquad m_{\theta\mid X}=\frac Xn.}
$$
Thus **the uniform-prior <posterior mode> is the <maximum-likelihood estimator>**, including $X=0,n$. The mean shrinks toward $1/2$ and equals the <maximum-likelihood estimator> only when $X=n/2$.

= ii
{parent=25j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <Jeffreys prior> is proportional to $[\theta(1-\theta)]^{-1/2}$, hence is $\operatorname{Beta}(1/2,1/2)$. Its posterior is $\operatorname{Beta}(X+1/2,n-X+1/2)$, giving
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E[\theta\mid X]=\frac{X+1/2}{n+1},\qquad m_{\theta\mid X}=\begin{cases}0&X=0,\\(X-1/2)/(n-1)&0<X<n,\\1&X=n.\end{cases}}
$$
The interior case is used only when $n\geq2$. Generally neither rule equals $X/n$: the mean agrees only at $X=n/2$; the mode agrees at the endpoints and, in the interior, at $X=n/2$. For the small experiments $n=1,2$, all possible observations happen to be among these cases, so the whole mode rule then coincides with the <maximum-likelihood estimator>.

= iii
{parent=25j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Put $s=\sqrt n/2$. The posterior is $\operatorname{Beta}(X+s,n-X+s)$, and
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E[\theta\mid X]=\frac{X+\sqrt n/2}{n+\sqrt n},\qquad m_{\theta\mid X}=\frac{\max(X+s-1,0)}{\max(X+s,1)+\max(n-X+s,1)-2}.}
$$
The denominator is positive for $n\geq1$. For $n>4$, both parameters exceed one and the mode simplifies to $(X+s-1)/(n+\sqrt n-2)$. For $n=4$ this is the uniform-prior mode, exactly $X/n$; for $n=1,2$ the endpoint or symmetric cases also make the mode rule coincide with $X/n$. Otherwise it is generally a different rule. The <posterior mean> agrees with $X/n$ only at $X=n/2$.

To find the <minimax estimator> under <quadratic loss>, consider the <posterior mean> $\delta_s=(X+s)/(n+2s)$. Its <risk function> is
$$
R(\theta,\delta_s)=\frac{n\theta(1-\theta)+s^2(1-2\theta)^2}{(n+2s)^2}.
$$
For $s^2=n/4$, the numerator is identically $n/4$, so
$$
\boxed{R(\theta,\delta_s)=\frac1{4(\sqrt n+1)^2}\quad\text{for all }\theta.}
$$
This <constant-risk binomial proportion estimator> is the unique <Bayes estimator> for its proper beta prior under <quadratic loss>. A competing rule's maximum risk is at least its risk averaged over that prior, which is at least this Bayes risk. The constant-risk rule attains the bound, so **the <posterior mean> in (iii) is the unique minimax rule**. Consequently any coincident rule is minimax too: the mean in (i) is the same rule when $n=4$, and the mean in (ii) is the same rule when $n=1$. No <posterior mode> listed here equals that mean rule for every observation, since at $X=0$ its value is positive whereas their endpoint modes differ from it. This accounts for the small-$n$ coincidences as well as the general answer.

= 26K
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Optimization and Control}

= Solution
{parent=26K}

Write $V(x)=F(\pi,x)$. The <Bellman equation> implies $V(x)\geq r(x,u)+\beta\mathbb E[V(x_1)\mid x,u]$ for every available action. Under any competing policy, repeated conditioning gives
$$
V(x)\geq\mathbb E_{\pi'}\left[\sum_{t=0}^{n-1}\beta^tr(x_t,u_t)+\beta^nV(x_n)\right]\geq\mathbb E_{\pi'}\sum_{t=0}^{n-1}\beta^tr(x_t,u_t).
$$
The last inequality uses $V\geq0$. The <monotone convergence theorem> now gives **$V(x)\geq F(\pi',x)$**, even when $\beta\geq1$. If $V(x)=\infty$ the comparison is immediate; otherwise the nonnegative conditional bounds justify each finite induction step. This proves <Bellman comparison with nonnegative rewards and superunit discount> without a vanishing-terminal-term assumption.

For the unit-bet policy in this <gambler's ruin> problem, put $\beta=\sqrt{9/8}=3/(2\sqrt2)$. The value satisfies
$$
V_x=\beta\left(\frac13V_{x+1}+\frac23V_{x-1}\right),\qquad V_0=0,\quad V_{100}=100.
$$
The characteristic equation has the repeated root $\sqrt2$, so $V_x=(A+Bx)2^{x/2}$. The boundary values give
$$
\boxed{V_x=x\,2^{(x-100)/2}.}
$$
To justify that this finite recurrence solution is the expected reward despite $\beta>1$, let $Q$ be the transition matrix on transient states $1,\ldots,99$. The matrix $\beta Q$ is diagonally similar, with diagonal factors $2^{x/2}$, to the symmetric tridiagonal matrix with off-diagonal entries $1/2$. Its <eigenvalues> are $\cos(j\pi/100)$, $1\leq j\leq99$, with absolute value below one. Thus its <Neumann series> converges and yields exactly the absorbed-payoff expectation and the unique recurrence solution.

When $p_2=1/4$, at wealth $2$ bet $2$ once and then follow the unit-bet policy. Its expected value is $\beta V_4/4=\beta V_2>V_2$, since $V_4=4V_2$. Therefore **the unit-bet policy is not optimal**.

= 27K
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Stochastic Financial Models}

= i
{parent=27k}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

A standard <Brownian motion> has $B_0=0$, continuous paths almost surely, and independent increments with $B_t-B_s\sim N(0,t-s)$ for $0\leq s<t$. With a specified filtration, the increment is also independent of the information available at time $s$.

= ii
{parent=27k}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

In the <Black-Scholes model>, absence of arbitrage and completeness give the price as the discounted expectation under the <risk-neutral measure>. Under that measure,
$$
\log S_T=\log S_0+(r-\sigma^2/2)T+\sigma\sqrt T\,Z,\qquad Z\sim N(0,1).
$$
The physical drift $\mu$ therefore does not enter the option price. Put
$$
d_2=\frac{\log(S_0/K)+(r-\sigma^2/2)T}{\sigma\sqrt T},\qquad d_1=d_2+\sigma\sqrt T.
$$
For the standard <normal distribution> function $\Phi$, $\mathbb P(S_T>K)=\Phi(d_2)$. Completing the square in the Gaussian density also gives $\mathbb E[S_T1_{\{S_T>K\}}]=S_0e^{rT}\Phi(d_1)$. Expanding the call payoff into these two truncated moments yields
$$
\boxed{C_0=S_0\Phi(d_1)-Ke^{-rT}\Phi(d_2).}
$$

= iii
{parent=27k}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Expand the payoff as $(S_T-2K+K^2/S_T)1_{\{S_T>K\}}$. The same <risk-neutral measure> applies. For any real $p$, completing the square gives the <truncated lognormal moment>
$$
\mathbb E[S_T^p1_{\{S_T>K\}}]=S_0^pe^{prT+p(p-1)\sigma^2T/2}\Phi(d_2+p\sigma\sqrt T).
$$
Using $p=1,0,-1$ and discounting by $e^{-rT}$ gives
$$
\boxed{V_0=S_0\Phi(d_1)-2Ke^{-rT}\Phi(d_2)+\frac{K^2}{S_0}e^{-2rT+\sigma^2T}\Phi(d_2-\sigma\sqrt T).}
$$
The inverse-price moment explains both the extra volatility factor and the shifted normal argument in the last term.

= 28B
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Dynamical Systems}

= a
{parent=28b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The linearized perturbation obeys $\dot\eta=D\mathbf f(\mathbf X(t))\eta$, so its <fundamental matrix> satisfies
$$
\boxed{\dot\Phi=D\mathbf f(\mathbf X(t))\Phi,\qquad\Phi(0)=I.}
$$
The <Floquet multipliers> are the <eigenvalues> of the <monodromy matrix> $\Phi(T)$. Differentiating the orbit equation shows that $\dot{\mathbf X}(t)=\mathbf f(\mathbf X(t))$ is a solution of the variational equation. For a nonconstant <periodic orbit> this tangent solution is nonzero and periodic, giving one multiplier equal to $1$.

The <Liouville formula for a fundamental matrix> gives $(\det\Phi)'=(\operatorname{tr}D\mathbf f)\det\Phi$. Since $\det\Phi(0)=1$, the product of the two multipliers is $\exp(\int_0^T\nabla\cdot\mathbf f(\mathbf X(t))\,dt)$. The other multiplier is therefore **exactly this exponential**.

= b
{parent=28b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Use the nearly conserved oscillator <energy> $E=(\dot x^2+x^2)/2$. The <differential equation> gives
$$
\dot E=-\varepsilon(2\dot x^4+2x^3\dot x-4x^4\dot x^2-\dot x^2).
$$
For a leading-order orbit $x=A\cos\theta$, $\dot x=-A\sin\theta$, the cycle averages are $\langle\dot x^4\rangle=3A^4/8$, $\langle x^3\dot x\rangle=0$, $\langle x^4\dot x^2\rangle=A^6/16$, and $\langle\dot x^2\rangle=A^2/2$. The <energy balance method> therefore gives
$$
\langle\dot E\rangle=\frac\varepsilon4A^2(A^2-1)(A^2-2),\qquad\dot A=\frac\varepsilon4A(A^2-1)(A^2-2).
$$
The two nonzero simple roots give the leading <limit cycles> **$x\simeq\cos(t+\theta_0)$ and $x\simeq\sqrt2\cos(t+\theta_0)$**. Below amplitude $1$ the <energy> increases, between $1$ and $\sqrt2$ it decreases, and above $\sqrt2$ it increases. Hence **the amplitude-$1$ cycle is stable and the amplitude-$\sqrt2$ cycle unstable** for positive small $\varepsilon$.

The linearized averaged radial exponents are $-\varepsilon/2$ and $\varepsilon$, respectively, consistent with nontrivial <Floquet multipliers> $e^{-\pi\varepsilon+O(\varepsilon^2)}$ and $e^{2\pi\varepsilon+O(\varepsilon^2)}$. The nonzero derivatives at these averaged zeros also justify persistence of nearby cycles for sufficiently small $\varepsilon$.

= 29D
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Integrable Systems}

= a
{parent=29d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The flow of the <vector field> is obtained by solving
$$
\frac{d\widetilde x}{d\varepsilon}=\xi(\widetilde x,\widetilde u),\qquad\frac{d\widetilde u}{d\varepsilon}=\eta(\widetilde x,\widetilde u),\qquad(\widetilde x,\widetilde u)|_{\varepsilon=0}=(x,u).
$$
Define $g^\varepsilon(x,u)$ to be this solution at parameter $\varepsilon$. Uniqueness implies $g^0=\operatorname{id}$ and $g^{\varepsilon+\delta}=g^\varepsilon\circ g^\delta$ wherever both sides are defined: shifting the solution parameter gives the same initial-value problem. In particular $g^{-\varepsilon}$ is the local inverse. This constructs the <one-parameter group> generated by the <vector field>, generally as a local group if the flow is not complete.

= b
{parent=29d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

A <Lie point symmetry> is a one-parameter transformation of $(x,u)$ which maps the <graph of a function> solving the <differential equation> to another such <graph of a function>, wherever the transformed independent variable is invertible along the <graph of a function>. Its induced transformation on derivatives is the prolongation. Infinitesimally the criterion is $\operatorname{pr}^{(n)}V(\Delta)=0$ on $\Delta=0$. The derivative coefficients can be computed recursively from $\eta^{(0)}=\eta$ by $\eta^{(j)}=D_x\eta^{(j-1)}-u^{(j)}D_x\xi$. This expresses the requirement that the transformed derivatives still satisfy the equation.

= c
{parent=29d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

A homogeneous linear differential operator satisfies $L(e^\varepsilon u)=e^\varepsilon L(u)$, so the scaling $x\mapsto x$, $u\mapsto e^\varepsilon u$ preserves every homogeneous linear equation. Its infinitesimal generator is **$V=u\partial_u$**.

On a patch with $u\ne0$, take $s=\log|u|$, $t=x$. Then $V(s)=1$, $V(t)=0$. Set $w=ds/dt=u'/u$. Since $u''/u=w'+w^2$, division by $u$ reduces the second-order equation to the <Riccati equation>
$$
\boxed{\frac{dw}{dt}+w^2+p(t)w+q(t)=0.}
$$
Recover a nonzero solution by $u=C\exp(\int w\,dt)$, allowing the sign in $C$. The zero solution is separate, and intervals containing zeros of other solutions require separate coordinate patches. The order reduction occurs because the transformed equation depends on $s'$ but not on $s$ itself.

= 30E
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Partial Differential Equations}

= Solution
{parent=30E}

Write $(\phi_\varepsilon*f)(x)=\int\phi(y)f(x-\varepsilon y)\,dy$. Since $\int\phi=1$,
$$
|(\phi_\varepsilon*f)(x)-f(x)|\leq\int|\phi(y)|\,|f(x-\varepsilon y)-f(x)|\,dy.
$$
A <Schwartz function> is bounded and <uniformly continuous>. Given a tolerance, first choose $R$ so the tail integral of $|\phi|$ is small. On $|y|\leq R$, <uniform continuity> makes the difference uniformly small for every $x$ when $\varepsilon R$ is small. On the tail it is bounded by $2\|f\|_\infty$. This proves **<uniform convergence>**, without assuming $\phi\geq0$, and is the <approximate identity> argument.

For the radial regularization of the three-dimensional <fundamental solution of the Laplace equation>, direct differentiation gives
$$
-\Delta N_\varepsilon(x)=\frac{3\varepsilon^2}{4\pi(|x|^2+\varepsilon^2)^{5/2}}=\varepsilon^{-3}\phi(x/\varepsilon),\qquad\phi(x)=\frac3{4\pi}(1+|x|^2)^{-5/2}.
$$
The radial integral is $\int\phi=3\int_0^\infty r^2(1+r^2)^{-5/2}\,dr=1$. This even <approximate identity> therefore gives $\int(-\Delta N_\varepsilon)f\to f(0)$ for $f\in\mathcal S(\mathbb R^3)$.

The function $N=1/(4\pi|x|)$ is locally integrable and determines a <tempered distribution>. We have $0<N_\varepsilon\leq N$, and $N|\Delta f|$ is integrable near zero and infinity for a Schwartz test function. Integration by parts and the <dominated convergence theorem> give
$$
\langle-\Delta N,f\rangle=\lim_{\varepsilon\downarrow0}\int N_\varepsilon(-\Delta f)=\lim_{\varepsilon\downarrow0}\int(-\Delta N_\varepsilon)f=f(0).
$$
Thus **$-\Delta N=\delta_0$**, proving that $N$ is a <fundamental solution of a linear differential operator>. In this last part the test-function space is $\mathcal S(\mathbb R^3)$; the printed $\mathbb R^n$ in the three-dimensional integral is understood with $n=3$.

= 31A
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Principles of Quantum Mechanics}

= Solution
{parent=31A}

For <spin> $1/2$, **$\mathbf S=\hbar\boldsymbol\sigma/2$**. The <Pauli matrices> satisfy $\sigma_i\sigma_j=\delta_{ij}I+i\epsilon_{ijk}\sigma_k$. The antisymmetric term vanishes on contraction with $n_in_j$, and $|\mathbf n|=1$, so $(\mathbf n\cdot\boldsymbol\sigma)^2=I$. Separating the even and odd terms of the exponential series gives
$$
\boxed{e^{-i\theta\mathbf n\cdot\mathbf S/\hbar}=I\cos(\theta/2)-i(\mathbf n\cdot\boldsymbol\sigma)\sin(\theta/2).}
$$
For the single-particle <Hamiltonian>, <unitary time evolution> consequently acts as
$$
|\uparrow\rangle\mapsto\cos(\alpha t/2)|\uparrow\rangle-i\sin(\alpha t/2)|\downarrow\rangle,\qquad|\downarrow\rangle\mapsto\cos(\alpha t/2)|\downarrow\rangle-i\sin(\alpha t/2)|\uparrow\rangle.
$$
For two <spins> the joint eigenstates of total <angular momentum> are the <spin-one-half triplet state>
$$
|1,1\rangle=|\uparrow\uparrow\rangle,\quad |1,0\rangle=\frac{|\uparrow\downarrow\rangle+|\downarrow\uparrow\rangle}{\sqrt2},\quad |1,-1\rangle=|\downarrow\downarrow\rangle,
$$
with $J^2=2\hbar^2$ and $J_3=m\hbar$, and the <spin-one-half singlet state>
$$
|0,0\rangle=\frac{|\uparrow\downarrow\rangle-|\downarrow\uparrow\rangle}{\sqrt2},\qquad J^2=J_3=0.
$$
These follow by adding the two spin-$1/2$ angular momenta, or by direct action of the Pauli matrices.

The two terms of the specified <Hamiltonian> commute, so its evolution factorizes into rotations with opposite angles on the two tensor factors. The unique initial $J_3=\hbar$ state is $|\uparrow\uparrow\rangle$. With $c=\cos(\lambda t/2)$, $s=\sin(\lambda t/2)$, its evolved state is
$$
c^2|\uparrow\uparrow\rangle+s^2|\downarrow\downarrow\rangle+ics(|\uparrow\downarrow\rangle-|\downarrow\uparrow\rangle).
$$
The singlet amplitude is $i\sqrt2cs$, giving the requested probability
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(J^2=0)=2c^2s^2=\tfrac12\sin^2(\lambda t).}
$$

= 32A
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Applications of Quantum Mechanics}

= Solution
{parent=32A}

Normalize the incident plane wave to unit amplitude. The scattering asymptotics are $\psi(\mathbf r)\sim e^{ikz}+f(\theta)e^{ikr}/r$; the outgoing coefficient $f$ is the <scattering amplitude>. The general axisymmetric free solution for $r>0$ is
$$
\psi(r,\theta)=\sum_{\ell\geq0}\{a_\ell j_\ell(kr)+b_\ell n_\ell(kr)\}P_\ell(\cos\theta),
$$
using <spherical Bessel functions>, <spherical Neumann functions> and <Legendre polynomials>.

For a real short-range central potential, the exterior radial solution in each <partial wave> is proportional to $\cos\delta_\ell\,j_\ell-\sin\delta_\ell\,n_\ell$, whose large-$r$ form is $\sin(kr-\ell\pi/2+\delta_\ell)/(kr)$. This defines the <scattering phase shift>. Multiplying this combination by $(2\ell+1)i^\ell e^{i\delta_\ell}$ matches the incoming spherical component of the incident plane wave: the factor $e^{i\delta_\ell}$ cancels the shifted incoming phase. The outgoing coefficient instead acquires $e^{2i\delta_\ell}$. Subtracting the plane wave's outgoing component therefore gives
$$
\boxed{f(\theta)=\frac1{2ik}\sum_{\ell\geq0}(2\ell+1)(e^{2i\delta_\ell}-1)P_\ell(\cos\theta)=\frac1k\sum_{\ell\geq0}(2\ell+1)e^{i\delta_\ell}\sin\delta_\ell\,P_\ell(\cos\theta).}
$$
For the barrier's $s$-wave, the regular reduced radial function inside is $u(r)=A\sinh(\kappa r)$, with $\kappa=\sqrt{\gamma^2-k^2}$. Outside take $u(r)=B\sin(kr+\delta_0)$. Continuity of $u$ and its derivative at $a$ equates logarithmic derivatives:
$$
\kappa\coth(\kappa a)=k\cot(ka+\delta_0),\qquad\boxed{\frac{\tanh(\kappa a)}{\kappa a}=\frac{\tan(ka+\delta_0)}{ka}.}
$$
Use the branch with $\delta_0\to0$ as $k\to0$. The matching equation gives $\delta_0=k(\tanh(\gamma a)/\gamma-a)+o(k)$, so the $s$-wave <scattering amplitude> tends to
$$
\boxed{f\longrightarrow\frac{\tanh(\gamma a)-\gamma a}{\gamma}.}
$$
Its negative is the <repulsive spherical barrier scattering length>, positive for this repulsive barrier.

= 33C
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Statistical Physics}

= a
{parent=33c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <Bose-Einstein distribution> gives $n_i=[\exp((E_i-\mu)/(k_BT))-1]^{-1}$, with <chemical potential> below the lowest <energy> when its discrete occupation is finite. In the continuum approximation,
$$
\boxed{N=\int_0^\infty\frac{g(E)}{e^{(E-\mu)/(k_BT)}-1}\,dE,\qquad U=\int_0^\infty\frac{Eg(E)}{e^{(E-\mu)/(k_BT)}-1}\,dE.}
$$
If the zero-energy <ground state> is macroscopically occupied, its discrete occupation $N_0$ must be added to the first integral; it contributes no <energy> to the second. This distinction is essential when applying the formulas to <Bose-Einstein condensation>.

= b
{parent=33c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <photon> number in thermal <blackbody radiation> is not fixed, so **$\mu=0$**. With $x=E/(k_BT)$,
$$
U=AV(k_BT)^4\int_0^\infty\frac{x^3}{e^x-1}\,dx=AV(k_BT)^4\frac{\pi^4}{15}.
$$
The integral is a temperature-independent finite constant (its explicit value follows from the gamma-zeta integral). Differentiating at fixed volume gives
$$
\boxed{C_V=\frac{4\pi^4}{15}AVk_B^4T^3,\qquad\alpha=3.}
$$

= c
{parent=33c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For fixed particle number, <Bose-Einstein condensation> means that a macroscopic fraction occupies the single-particle <ground state>. As $\mu\uparrow0$, the excited-state population reaches its maximum
$$
N_{\rm ex}=B(k_BT)^3\int_0^\infty\frac{x^2}{e^x-1}\,dx=2B\zeta(3)(k_BT)^3.
$$
At the transition this equals $N$, so
$$
\boxed{T_c=\frac1{k_B}\left(\frac{N}{2B\zeta(3)}\right)^{1/3}\propto N^{1/3}.}
$$
Below $T_c$, the <chemical potential> is approximately zero in the large-$N$ continuum description. Additional particles occupy the <ground state>, giving
$$
\boxed{\frac{N_0}{N}\simeq1-\left(\frac T{T_c}\right)^3,\qquad\gamma=3.}
$$
For finite $N$, the actual <chemical potential> remains slightly negative; the saturation statement is the macroscopic approximation used here.

= d
{parent=33c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

**Ordinary equilibrium blackbody <photons> do not undergo this fixed-number condensation.** Their number adjusts through absorption and emission, while their <chemical potential> stays zero; cooling reduces the equilibrium <photon> number rather than forcing an excess into the <ground state>. This answer concerns the thermal blackbody model, not specially constrained systems where an effective <photon> number is maintained.

= 34D
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=General Relativity}

= a
{parent=34d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Let $f(r)=1-r_s/r$ and parametrize the <timelike geodesic> by <proper time> $\tau$. Time-translation and rotational symmetry conserve $E=f\dot t$ and $h=r^2\dot\phi$ in the equatorial plane. The normalized <four-velocity> obeys
$$
-1=-f\dot t^2+f^{-1}\dot r^2+r^2\dot\phi^2.
$$
Substitution and multiplication by $f$ give $\dot r^2+f(1+h^2/r^2)=E^2$, or
$$
\boxed{\tfrac12\dot r^2+V(r)=\tfrac12E^2,\qquad2V=(1-r_s/r)(1+h^2/r^2).}
$$
The dot denotes a proper-time derivative, and $E,h$ are <energy> and <angular momentum> per unit rest mass.

= b
{parent=34d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

At a circular orbit, $V'=0$. Differentiating the Schwarzschild <effective potential> at fixed $h$ gives
$$
V'=\frac{r_s}{2r^2}-\frac{h^2}{r^3}+\frac{3r_sh^2}{2r^4},\qquad\boxed{h^2=\frac{r^2r_s}{2r-3r_s}.}
$$
A timelike circular orbit requires $r>3r_s/2$. Evaluating the second derivative after this substitution gives
$$
V''=\frac{r_s(r-3r_s)}{r^3(2r-3r_s)}.
$$
A stable nondegenerate minimum therefore requires **$r>3r_s$**. The boundary $r=3r_s$ is the marginal <innermost stable circular orbit>, not a strict stable minimum.

= c
{parent=34d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For Alice's circular geodesic, the conserved quantities or radial geodesic equation give $\Omega=d\phi/dt=\sqrt{r_s/(2r^3)}$. Consequently
$$
\left(\frac{d\tau_A}{dt}\right)^2=f-r^2\Omega^2=1-\frac{3r_s}{2r}.
$$
Bob at rest far away has $d\tau_B/dt\simeq1$. Thus the ratio of ageing rates is
$$
\boxed{k^2=\frac1{1-3r_s/(2r)}=\frac{2r}{2r-3r_s}.}
$$
For Alice's stable orbit $r>3r_s$, the denominator factor exceeds $1/2$, proving **$k<\sqrt2$**. Both gravitational time dilation and Alice's orbital motion enter this proper-time ratio.

= 35E
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Fluid Dynamics II}

= i
{parent=35e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Near the closest point, in $X=x-Ut$, the gap is $h(X)\simeq h_0+X^2/(2a)$. The horizontal length scale is **$\ell=\sqrt{2ah_0}$**, up to an inessential constant. Thus the aspect ratio $h_0/\ell\ll1$ follows from $h_0\ll a$. The <lubrication approximation> assumes an incompressible <Newtonian fluid>, no slip on the surfaces, slow variation of the gap, and negligible inertia relative to transverse viscous stress:
$$
\boxed{\frac{\rho U h_0^2}{\mu\ell}\ll1.}
$$
This is the reduced Reynolds-number condition; it is weaker than requiring a <Reynolds number> based on $\ell$ to be small. In the cylinder frame the geometry and flow are steady. <Pressure> is uniform across the narrow gap to leading order, and equal ambient <pressures> far to either side close the model. The calculation assumes a full liquid film; <cavitation> would change that <pressure> condition.

= ii
{parent=35e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Reflection about the closest point leaves the gap geometry unchanged and reverses the direction of translation. The linear Stokes equations make the excess <pressure> change sign when the imposed <velocity> changes sign. Combining reflection with this linearity shows that <pressure> is odd in $X$. Its leading normal force on the stationary plane therefore cancels between the two sides, giving **$\mathbf e_y\cdot\mathbf F=0$ in the lubrication limit**. Normal viscous stresses are of smaller order in the aspect ratio. This uses symmetry only, rather than a <pressure> calculation.

= iii
{parent=35e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

In the laboratory frame the lower wall has <velocity> zero and the cylinder surface has <velocity> $U$. Solving $\mu u_{yy}=p_X$ with these no-slip conditions gives
$$
\boxed{u(X,y)=\frac{Uy}{h}+\frac{p_X}{2\mu}y(y-h).}
$$
The relative <velocity> in the cylinder frame is $u-U$. Its flux is constant by incompressibility:
$$
q=-\frac{Uh}{2}-\frac{h^3p_X}{12\mu}.
$$
Equal <pressures> at the two infinities require $\int p_X\,dX=0$. Put $I_j=\int h^{-j}\,dX$; then $q=-(U/2)I_2/I_3$. With $X=\ell s$ and $h=h_0(1+s^2)$, the given integrals yield $I_2/I_3=4h_0/3$. Hence $q=-2Uh_0/3$ and
$$
\boxed{p_X=\frac{2\mu U(4h_0-3h)}{h^3}.}
$$
Indeed $p=2\mu UX/h^2$ has this derivative and tends to ambient <pressure> at both ends. The distinction between the constant comoving flux and the varying laboratory flux is necessary because the gap translates.

= iv
{parent=35e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

The fluid's tangential traction on the lower stationary surface is $\tau=\mu u_y|_{y=0}$ in the positive $x$ direction. Using the <pressure> gradient found above gives
$$
\tau=\frac{\mu U}{h}-\frac h2p_X=4\mu U\left(\frac1h-\frac{h_0}{h^2}\right).
$$
The <drag on a plane beneath a translating cylinder> follows by integrating along the wall:
$$
\boxed{\mathbf e_x\cdot\mathbf F=4\mu U\frac\ell{h_0}\left(\pi-\frac\pi2\right)=2\pi\mu U\sqrt{\frac{2a}{h_0}}}
$$
per unit cylinder-axis length. This is the force on the lower surface, so its direction follows the cylinder's translation.

= 36B
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Waves}

= Solution
{parent=36B}

The linear elastic wave equation is $\rho\mathbf u_{tt}=(\lambda+\mu)\nabla(\nabla\cdot\mathbf u)+\mu\Delta\mathbf u$. For a <shear-horizontal wave>, take $\mathbf u=(0,v(x,z,t),0)$ with no $y$ dependence. Its divergence vanishes, and
$$
v_{tt}=c_s^2(v_{xx}+v_{zz}),\qquad c_s=\sqrt{\mu/\rho}.
$$
Rigid fixed boundaries require $v=0$ at $z=0,h$. The modes are $v=A\sin(q_nz)\cos(kx-\omega_nt)$ with $q_n=n\pi/h$, $n=1,2,\ldots$, and
$$
\boxed{\omega_n(k)=c_s\sqrt{k^2+q_n^2},\qquad\omega_{n,\mathrm{cut}}=c_sq_n.}
$$
There is no $n=0$ displacement mode with these clamped boundary conditions. For $k>0$, the <phase velocity> and <group velocity> are
$$
\boxed{c(k)=c_s\frac{\sqrt{k^2+q_n^2}}k,\qquad c_g(k)=c_s\frac{k}{\sqrt{k^2+q_n^2}}.}
$$
The <phase velocity> decreases from infinity to $c_s$, while the <group velocity> increases from zero to $c_s$; their product is $c_s^2$.

\Image[paper-2-sh-waveguide.png]
{title=Dispersion, phase velocity and group velocity of a clamped shear-horizontal waveguide mode}

For a mode of frequency $\omega$, the appropriate time and cross-sectional average is $\langle A\rangle=\omega/(2\pi h)\int_0^{2\pi/\omega}\int_0^h A\,dz\,dt$, per unit transverse width. Its average <kinetic energy density> is $\rho A^2\omega^2/8$. The only nonzero strains are $e_{xy}=e_{yx}=v_x/2$ and $e_{yz}=e_{zy}=v_z/2$, while $e_{kk}=0$. Thus the elastic <energy> density is $\mu(v_x^2+v_z^2)/2$, whose average is $\mu A^2(k^2+q_n^2)/8$. The <dispersion relation> proves **equality of average kinetic and elastic energies**.

For the <ray asymptotics of a clamped elastic waveguide mode>, expand the localized initial displacement in transverse sine modes and <Fourier transform> in $x$. For a displacement released from rest, each mode evolves with $\cos(\omega_n(k)t)$. At $x=Vt$ the oscillatory phases are $t(kV\pm\omega_n(k))$. If $0<|V|<c_s$, one phase has a <stationary point> with
$$
|k_*|=\frac{q_n|V|}{\sqrt{c_s^2-V^2}},\qquad\omega_n''(k_*)>0.
$$
The <stationary phase method> gives a **generic oscillatory amplitude proportional to $t^{-1/2}$** for each contributing mode. If the initial <Fourier coefficient> vanishes at the <stationary point>, that mode can decay faster; smooth localized data make the modal sum well behaved. If $|V|>c_s$, there is no <stationary point>. Repeated integration by parts gives rapid decay for smooth rapidly decaying data; for initially compactly supported displacement, <finite propagation speed> makes the displacement on that ray **exactly zero after a sufficiently long time**. These are the requested subsonic and super-shear-speed regimes.

= 37E
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Numerical Analysis}

= a
{parent=37e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Order the interior grid values into a vector. The coefficient matrix has diagonal $4+ch^2$ and off-diagonal entries $-1$ at horizontal and vertical neighbours, with boundary values already zero. Every row is strictly diagonally dominant because $c>0$. The standard convergence theorem for <strict diagonal dominance> gives **convergence of both the <Jacobi method> and the <Gauss-Seidel method>**. The matrix is also symmetric positive definite.

For $D=(4+ch^2)I$, the <weighted Jacobi method> is $u^{(r+1)}=u^{(r)}+\omega D^{-1}(b-Au^{(r)})$, with iteration matrix $H_\omega=I-\omega D^{-1}A$. The sine-product grid <eigenvectors> give
$$
\boxed{\lambda_{k,l}=1-\omega\frac{4\{\sin^2(\pi kh/2)+\sin^2(\pi lh/2)\}+ch^2}{4+ch^2},\qquad1\leq k,l\leq m.}
$$
These <weighted Jacobi eigenvalues for a square Dirichlet grid> determine convergence. All <eigenvalues> of $A$ are positive. Thus for fixed $h$ convergence requires $0<\omega<2(4+ch^2)/a_{\max}$, where $a_{\max}=8\cos^2(\pi h/2)+ch^2$. As $h\downarrow0$, this upper bound tends to $1$ from above. Any fixed $\omega>1$ therefore fails on sufficiently fine grids; $\omega\leq0$ also fails. Consequently **$0<\omega\leq1$ is necessary for all mesh sizes**, and it is sufficient, since $a_{\max}<2(4+ch^2)$ for every finite grid.

= b
{parent=37e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

A <multigrid method> combines a fine-grid smoother with a <coarse-grid correction>. Starting with $u_h$, apply a few smoothing iterations, compute $r_h=b_h-A_hu_h$, restrict this residual to a coarser grid, and approximately solve $A_He_H=Rr_h$. Prolong the coarse error and update $u_h\leftarrow u_h+Pe_H$, then post-smooth. The coarse solve is itself performed recursively, with a direct solve at the coarsest level; visiting each coarser level once gives a V-cycle. The smoother damps rapidly varying error, while error smooth on the fine grid is efficiently represented and corrected on the coarse grid.

For ordinary Jacobi, $\omega=1$, the highest-frequency sine-product error has <eigenvalue> approaching $-1$, not zero. In particular for the discrete Poisson equation it is $-\cos(\pi h)$, whose magnitude approaches one. This nearly alternating error changes sign between sweeps but is barely damped, and ordinary coarse-grid restriction does not represent it well. Thus **with the standard point-Jacobi smoother and coarse-grid transfers one chooses $\omega\ne1$, usually $0<\omega<1$, for fast multigrid convergence**. For example $\omega=2/3$ makes the extreme high-frequency <eigenvalue> approach $-1/3$. Convergence of Jacobi as a standalone iteration is therefore insufficient to guarantee that it is an effective smoother. This conclusion concerns that standard multigrid choice; other smoothers or specially designed transfers can change it.
